<p>Bangladesh is India's largest onion destination by a wide margin — verified WITS/UN Comtrade data puts it at USD 209.5 million in 2023, roughly a third of India's total onion export value worldwide, well ahead of the next-largest markets (Malaysia at USD 89.3 million, the UAE at USD 81.5 million). That scale reflects pure proximity economics: short transit and minimal freight cost make Indian onion structurally the cheapest reliable option for Bangladeshi buyers. This guide is for importers setting up or re-pricing a Bangladesh onion programme: how to specify grade and packing, what each side files, how India's export policy feeds into your price, and where first orders go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<h2>Specification and packing</h2>
<p>Specify a size grade explicitly — 40-60mm, 50-70mm or 60-80mm — since preferences differ by buyer segment even within Bangladesh. Packing is mesh or net bags (5-25kg) for airflow; properly cured onion in ventilated packaging is what survives the trip in good condition, and sealed formats are not something we'd recommend regardless of what packaging a buyer's retail programme might otherwise prefer.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The export-policy line in the checklist is the one that changes most: whatever MEP, duty or licensing condition is in force on the day of shipment is what the paperwork reflects. On the Bangladesh side, BSTI clearance involves mandatory testing on arrival, separate from the phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin we provide, and the Plant Quarantine Wing import permit applies because this is plant-origin fresh produce — build both into your first-shipment timeline. See our <a href="/markets/bangladesh/">Bangladesh market guide</a> for the full documentation and payment-norms picture.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Onion is volatile even by fresh-produce standards, and we don't publish a flat rate because the market genuinely doesn't move on one. Four things matter most: the size and timing of the Nashik/Maharashtra rabi harvest (the crop that dominates export volume and stores well — a weak or delayed harvest tightens supply fast), India's own domestic price-stabilisation interventions (a Minimum Export Price sets a legal floor, an export duty adds a direct cost, and — as 2023-24 showed — outright export bans are a real historical risk, not a hypothetical one), the size grade you specify (larger grades generally command a premium), and which route you use (sea to Chattogram vs. overland via Petrapole-Benapole have different cost structures, see the comparison below).</p>
<p>We'd rather explain the policy side clearly than have you discover it mid-negotiation. India actively manages onion exports to protect domestic supply and consumer prices — using a minimum export price, at times an export duty, and, from December 2023 to May 2024, an outright export ban of almost five months. These measures have changed with limited notice more than once, and reports suggest the export duty was removed again in April 2025 — which is itself exactly the kind of change that can happen with little warning. So we don't quote onion business as though policy conditions were fixed: when you place an order, we confirm the applicable terms at that point — MEP, any duty, licensing requirements — and your contract reflects what's actually in force. If you're building a recurring Bangladesh onion programme, plan for that variability explicitly rather than assuming a stable baseline.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific grade, packaging, route and destination — and always against the export-policy conditions in force at the time of quoting, never a projection of future policy. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: size grade, quantity, preferred route (sea or land), and your target delivery window.</p>
